   "notes": "Slide 5 of 49. BERT = masked language model: predict a hidden token from both sides; scored by pseudo-likelihood. GPT = autoregressive (causal) model: predict each token from those before it in a chosen order; the chain rule gives an exact likelihood for any order. The two share no intrinsic metric, so they are compared only on downstream results (closure, AUC after fine-tuning, gain as NN inputs). Labels follow goal set mk2 §2: count models report a pseudo-likelihood (BERT-like scoring); the Chow–Liu tree is autoregressive with one parent per token. Synergistic features (feature-selection notes, 5 Nov): by the chain rule, a feature with I(Y;Z) = 0 can still add I(Y;Z|X) > 0. Three mechanisms: synergy (information only in the joint distribution, the synergy term of a partial information decomposition), context or conditioning variables, and suppressor variables (Guyon and Elisseeff, JMLR 3 (2003) 1157). The current NN1/NN2 lists keep features with strong or medium separation, so the selection removes synergistic and context features first; a grammar is structure in p(x), the relations among features, which LEXICON models directly. Such features also bring detector and MC-only structure, so any gain needs the nuisance and data/MC checks (F13–F17). A positive finding (a significant held-out drop under class-conditional permutation) can be established; a negative only as 'no effect above epsilon'."
